    "translation": "In the science museum there is a hall about dikes, pumps and salt water creeping inland. Visitors can turn a wheel that shows how much force is needed to keep a polder dry. Guide Noor notices that children like the wheel more than the text panels, although the panels are precisely what explain the numbers. An interactive screen compares a dry summer with a wet winter. Noor hears parents say they thought the Netherlands only had too much water, because the news often covers floods. The screen also shows that some crops actually need extra irrigation. At the end visitors may write a card about what they want to do differently at home. Many people mention shorter showers; a few write about green roofs. Noor keeps the cards for a school project so that pupils can read real sentences from visitors. The water hall is on a higher floor, and children stay longest at the wheel. The screen compares a wet winter month with a dry summer and also shows salt creeping inland. Noor keeps the cards for a school class that can read real visitor sentences about showers and green roofs.",
