    "translation": "After a tendon injury to her Achilles tendon Roos had to rest for six weeks, without the weekly Sunday group run. The physiotherapist gave towel exercises and banned hills, although Roos especially loves forest routes. She stuck to that because she did not want to miss a spring race in April. In week seven she was allowed to jog-walk: two minutes of easy running, then one minute of walking, on a flat path along the canal. An app kept the times so that she would not speed up too early if she felt better. Her boyfriend ran along and deliberately talked slowly, which kept her pace low without him constantly saying ‘slower’. When the pain came back once after a wet session, she stopped immediately and emailed the practice with a short description of where it stabbed. The therapist prescribed an extra day of rest and a different warm-up with more time for the calves. Roos learned that perseverance here is not the same as keeping running until the tendon protests again. The spring race is in April over ten kilometres along the canal. In week seven Roos trained on a flat towpath, with an alarm in the app against running too fast. After the wet session the practice emailed quickly: still no hills, and more time for the calves in the warm-up.",
