    "translation": "For her teacher-training degree Inés is doing a three-day-a-week placement in year six of a primary school in Tilburg, between maths and world orientation. The mentor first lets her lead small circles, because a whole class still gives too many stimuli and she does not yet know all the names. Inés prepares a lesson on maps, although she herself still gets nervous about the interactive whiteboard when the connection stalls. During the lesson a pupil got confused about north and south on the school map that hung on the wall. Inés walked with him to the window and pointed out the playground, which suddenly made the map understandable because he recognised the slide. The mentor later wrote that that movement was stronger than yet another explanation from the board with arrows. After school Inés briefly evaluates what took too long, usually the start with too much explanation. She shortens the start and plans more time for questions from pupils who speak softly. Her report for college now contains not only theory from the handbook, but also this concrete adjustment. The primary school has a full year-six class, and Inés does three long placement days. Her first lesson on maps had too much explanation and too little practice. In the placement report she describes how the route via the window did more than arrows on the whiteboard.",
