    "translation": "In the distribution centre the bonus for night shifts was higher than that for irregular evening hours, so the same people voluntarily chose the heaviest rotas. The occupational physician noticed more errors in week four of a night block, not in week one. Management read the bonus as employee preference; the physician read it as an incentive that undermines health. The works council demanded a maximum of three consecutive night weeks and a compulsory return night to a day rhythm. HR feared understaffing in the peak towards the holidays. The compromise became a voluntary swap exchange plus a hard cap in the roster software, so that shift leaders could not bypass the limit by app. Error figures fell slightly, as did the bonus bill. Some workers grumbled about less extra money. The council said that was not proof the old system was better, only that an unhealthy incentive had been successful. The distribution centre in Nieuwegein pays a higher bonus on night shifts than on irregular evenings. The occupational physician counted more pick errors in week four of a night block than in week one, on a sample of crews who had voluntarily chosen the heaviest blocks. The works council demanded a cap of three consecutive night weeks and a return night to a day rhythm. HR pointed to the peak towards the holidays and feared understaffing if nobody chose four weeks in a row any longer. The roster software has since refused a fourth night week; the swap board in the canteen arranged voluntary swaps. The bonus bill fell, as did the errors. Less extra money thus mainly proved that the higher night bonus had worked as an incentive, not that the cap made the peak impossible.",
